---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteNav from "../components/SiteNav.astro";

// The setup offer page. Copy: issues/copy-pack-setup-offer.md section 6, approved by Marko
// 2026-11-20 with all four defaults (the LLC footer line, a working test command as a
// prerequisite, the founding price only, a thirty-minute call). The example is
// issues/ai-setup-offer-practice/case-study-draft.md in the monolith, approved the same day;
// its opening paragraph is left out here because the lede above already says it.
// Plain register on purpose (Marko, 2026-11-20): the expedition voice stays an accent on the
// home bands, the field journal and the Legend, not on the page that asks for money.
// Layout and night tokens follow call.astro, the page this one sends readers to.

const PR_URL = "https://github.com/QuackbackIO/quackback/pull/1232";
const FORK_URL = "https://github.com/marko-builds/quackback/tree/ai-setup";

const pageTitle = "Claude Code, set up in your team's repo | Marko Stankovic";
const pageDescription =
  "A fixed-price Claude Code setup in your own repo: rules it reads, an interview before it plans, tests before code, and a review aimed at what agents get wrong. EUR 1,200, two weeks.";
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <style is:global>
    /* Night register, the same body-level token remap as call.astro. */
    body {
      --color-bg: var(--color-night-bg);
      --color-surface-veil: var(--color-night-surface);
      --color-surface-raised: var(--color-night-surface);
      --color-border: var(--color-night-border);
      --color-border-strong: var(--color-night-border);
      --color-text-primary: var(--color-night-text);
      --color-text-secondary: var(--color-night-text-secondary);
      --color-text-muted: var(--color-night-text-secondary);
      --color-accent: var(--color-aurora-cyan);
      --color-accent-dim: var(--color-night-border);
      --color-grid: var(--color-night-border);
      background-color: var(--color-night-bg);
    }
  </style>

  <SiteNav active="setup" />

  <main class="page">
    <div class="head band">
      <div class="frame">
        <p class="entry-line">Setup</p>
        <h1>Claude Code, set up in your team's repo</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          For small product teams already paying for Claude Code who get uneven results: some
          days a clean fix, other days the wrong thing built well and tests that agree with its
          own bug.
        </p>
        <a class="btn" href="/call">Book a call</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="body band">
      <div class="frame">
        <section class="sec" id="what">
          <h2>What you get</h2>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>
              <strong>A <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and path rules</strong> written from an interview about
              your codebase: your commands, your conventions, the mistakes an agent makes in your code.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The chain:</strong> an interview before anything is planned, a short PRD,
              vertical-slice issues an agent can pick up, and test-first implementation.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A review pass</strong> with two reviewers: one checks the diff against your
              rules, one checks it against its issue.
            </li>
            <li><strong>One ticket of yours</strong> taken through the whole chain, with me, in the final session.</li>
            <li><strong>14 days of written follow-up</strong> after handover.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">Everything is plain files in your repo. You own it when I leave.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="sec" id="how">
          <h2>How it runs</h2>
          <p class="prose">
            Two weeks, remote. A kickoff interview about your code and your pain points, a read of
            the codebase, the rules written and reviewed with you, the chain installed, one real
            ticket from interview to merged, then handover. It runs on your team's Claude account,
            never mine.
          </p>
          <p class="prose">
            Day to day, your team starts each step by asking Claude for it ("grill me on this",
            "review the diff"), and a short guide in the repo says which steps a change needs.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="sec" id="price">
          <h2>Price</h2>
          <p class="prose">
            <strong>EUR 1,200</strong>, the founding price, for a team that agrees to a case study
            (named or anonymised, your choice, and you approve it before it goes anywhere). Half
            upfront, half at handover.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="sec" id="need">
          <h2>You need</h2>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>Claude Code seats for the people who will use it.</li>
            <li>A test command that runs. The test-first loop needs one.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="sec" id="excluded">
          <h2>Not included</h2>
          <p class="prose">
            Building features beyond the one ticket, security or compliance guarantees, tools other
            than Claude Code, unattended agent loops, and support after the 14 days (that is a new
            quote).
          </p>
          <a class="btn" href="/call">Book a call</a>
        </section>

        <section class="sec" id="example">
          <h2>An example: Quackback</h2>
          <p class="prose">
            To show what that looks like, I ran it on Quackback, an open-source customer feedback
            platform (TypeScript, Bun, 56 business-logic domains, about 790 merged PRs). No client,
            no prior context, timed from the first minute.
          </p>

          <h3>Time</h3>
          <p class="prose">
            51 minutes from first command to an open upstream PR, about 30 of them mine at the
            keyboard. With a team, add the kickoff interview, a review of the rules with you, and one
            live session on your own ticket. I expect most of the hours to go there; the first
            engagement's log will show it.
          </p>

          <h3>What went into the repo</h3>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>
              <strong><code>CLAUDE.md</code></strong>, written from reading the code and CI rather
              than from a template: the exact commands CI runs, which tests need Postgres and which
              run bare, where business logic and endpoints live, the commit style the last 500
              commits follow.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Two path rules:</strong> one for server code (typed errors, constant-time
              comparisons for secrets), one for tests (add the refused case next to every accepted
              one when a fix touches security).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Five skills</strong> that take a change from idea to reviewed diff: an
              alignment interview, a PRD, vertical-slice issues, test-first implementation, and a
              two-reviewer diff review.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="prose">Two things the read turned up that a template would have missed:</p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>
              The repo's <code>.gitignore</code> excluded all of <code>.claude/</code>. Any shared
              setup committed there would have stayed on one laptop with no error. The fix shares
              rules and skills and keeps personal settings local. The installer now checks for this
              on every repo.
            </li>
            <li>
              Installed with a newer Bun than the one the repo pins, typecheck reported thousands of
              errors in files nobody had touched. With the pinned version: zero. That is now one
              line in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so no agent burns an afternoon on it.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3>One open issue from their tracker, through the whole chain</h3>
          <p class="prose">
            Issue #818, reported by a user: a customer replies to a support email from Yahoo Mail,
            Yahoo lowercases the reply address, the signed tag in it no longer matches, and the
            reply opens a new conversation instead of threading.
          </p>
          <ul class="list">
            <li>
              <strong>Interview:</strong> three questions and a confirmation settled the approach
              (compare case-insensitively, so every address already sent keeps working), how far to
              fold, and what the PR would contain.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Tests first:</strong> each behaviour started as a failing test. The refusal
              test was checked by removing the comparison from the verifier and watching it fail.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Review:</strong> the reviewer checking the diff against its issue found a real
              bug in the first version. Full Unicode case folding turns the Kelvin sign into an
              ASCII <code>k</code>, so a look-alike address could verify. It became a failing test,
              then an ASCII-only fold.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Result:</strong> 89 tests pass, lint and typecheck clean, opened upstream as
              <a class="inline-link" href={PR_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">QuackbackIO/quackback#1232</a>.
              The setup itself is on <a class="inline-link" href={FORK_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a public fork</a>.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="metric">PR #1232: open, CLA signed, waiting on the maintainer.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="sec" id="who">
          <h2>Who I am</h2>
          <p class="prose">
            I'm Marko, a software engineer in Belgrade. Three years of Unity and C#, then Replai, a
            live product, until September 2025. More on
            <a class="inline-link" href="/about">the about page</a>, code at
            <a class="inline-link" href="https://github.com/marko-builds" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/marko-builds</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="sec" id="book">
          <h2>Book a call</h2>
          <p class="prose">Thirty minutes about your repo and what goes wrong in it. English or Serbian.</p>
          <a class="btn" href="/call">Book a call</a>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <footer class="band">
    <div class="frame foot">
      <p>&copy; 2026 Marko Stankovic. Services contracted and invoiced by Monolithiq LLC.</p>
      <a class="foot-link" href="/">back to home</a>
    </div>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Same frame, head, section and button rules as call.astro, so the two pages read as one
     flow. Tokens are global.css's; nothing here is a literal. */
  .band { padding-inline: 3.5rem; }
  .frame { max-width: 62.5rem; margin-inline: auto; }

  .head { padding-block: 8rem 3rem; }

  .entry-line {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display, var(--font-sans));
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .lede {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.625;
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
  }

  .body { padding-block: 3.5rem 6rem; }

  .sec > h2::before { content: none; }
  .sec > h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display, var(--font-sans));
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  /* global.css sets h3 to mono unlayered; the display face is written here for that reason. */
  .sec h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display, var(--font-sans));
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .sec h3::before { content: none; }
  .sec + .sec {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    padding-top: 3rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .prose,
  .note,
  .list {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 42em;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  .note { color: var(--color-text-primary); }
  .list { padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; }
  .list strong,
  .prose strong { color: var(--color-text-primary); font-weight: 600; }
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  .metric {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
  }

  /* Buttons: outline on the night border, aurora cyan on hover, as on call.astro. */
  .btn {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    background: transparent;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
    padding: 0.62rem 1.4rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 0.15s ease, border-color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
  }
  .btn:hover {
    color: var(--color-night-bg);
    background: var(--color-aurora-cyan);
    border-color: var(--color-aurora-cyan);
  }

  footer { border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .foot {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding-block: 2rem;
  }
  .foot p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .foot-link {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 0.15s ease;
  }
  .foot-link:hover { color: var(--color-text-secondary); }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .band { padding-inline: 24px; }
    .head { padding-block: 6.5rem 2.5rem; }
    .body { padding-block: 2.5rem 4rem; }
    .btn { min-height: 44px; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .btn, .foot-link { transition: none; }
  }
</style>
